StillGood is a food waste app that I started in April 2026. Users can snap a photo of their groceries, get reminded before the items expire and receive recipe suggestions to use them up. I designed and developed the whole product on my own, including the mobile app, the backend server and the website.

To make this possible as a solo developer, I worked with Claude Code, an AI coding assistant. In this post, I want to share how that actually worked in practice.

## I Decide, the AI Types

The split between me and the AI was quite simple. I decided how the app should work: what screens it has, what each screen does and how the data moves between the app and the server. Claude Code then wrote a lot of the code to make that happen, much faster than I could have typed it myself.

What made this work was giving the AI clear written rules to follow, and treating every change it made like one from a new teammate. Each change went through a pull request, and I read through and tested it myself before it was merged.

## The Tech Stack

The app is built with React Native and Expo, with a Fastify server and a Supabase database behind it. Google's Gemini AI reads the grocery photos and suggests recipes, while PostHog shows me how users actually use the app so I can make better decisions when improving it.

## My Advice for Anyone Starting Out

An AI assistant makes you faster, but it does not finish the work for you. The idea, the design, the testing and the launch were all still my responsibility, and so was catching every mistake before it reached my users.

StillGood went live on the Google Play Store on 26 August 2026. Feel free to download it and let me know what you think!
